    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Using Corollary <a href="/tag/B7A1FF" data-tag="B7A1FF">4.2.1.8</a>, we can identify the mapping space $\bHom _{\Fun (K,\calC ) }( p, \delta (Y) )$ with the fiber $\calC ^{p/} \times _{\calC } \{ Y\} $, for each object $Y \in \calC $. Under this identification, the map $\phi _{Y}$ can be identified with the fiber over $Y$ of the composition 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \calC ^{X/} \stackrel{\phi '}{\rightarrow } \calC ^{ \overline{p}/ } \stackrel{\phi ''}{\rightarrow } \calC ^{p/}, \]
</div>
    <p>
       where $\phi '$ is a section to the trivial fibration $\calC ^{\overline{p}/} \rightarrow \calC ^{X/}$. The map $\phi ''$ is a left fibration (Proposition <a href="/tag/0100E2" data-tag="0100E2">4.2.1.6</a>). Condition $(i)$ is equivalent to the requirement that $\phi ''$ be a trivial Kan fibration, and condition $(ii)$ is equivalent to the requirement that each of the maps 
    <p>
      <div class="equation">
  \[  \phi ''_{Y}: \calC ^{ \overline{p}/} \times _{\calC } \{ Y \}  \rightarrow \calC ^{p/} \times _{\calC } \{ Y\} . \]
</div>
    <p>
       is a homotopy equivalence of Kan compexes (which, in view of Lemma <a href="/tag/B732E8" data-tag="B732E8">2.1.3.3</a>, is equivalent to the requirement that $\phi ''_{Y}$ be a trivial Kan fibration). The equivalence of these two conditions now follows from Lemma <a href="/tag/0628D7" data-tag="0628D7">2.1.3.4</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
